\f1\fs22 Kathy\'92s invitation arrived the way corporate disasters always did: politely, with no context, and timed to coincide with being halfway through something important, like applying a patch or pretending she hadn\'92t.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Her tablet chimed once\'97an expensive, tasteful sound that suggested the device had been raised better than she had. The notification banner read:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs18 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>AUDIENCE REQUESTED  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>OFFICE OF THE HIGH ELVIS  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>IMMEDIATE  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>ATTENDEES: HOLY MOMMA, AUNT KATHY, AUNT HAYLEY, SEVEN SAGES CORP\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>ESCORT: PROVIDED  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>NOTES: NO GOSPEL SONGS
\fs20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs22 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy stared at the last line until it stared back.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No Gospel Songs,\'94 she said aloud to the empty kitchen, like she was tasting the words for poison. \'93Righto.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What is it?\'94 Sarah asked, as she came in.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy tilted the tablet so Sarah could read it. She watched Sarah\'92s face change on the last line.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Is that a joke?\'94 Sarah said quietly. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley, who had been leaning against the doorframe with the casual posture of someone whose job was to be underestimated, glanced at the screen and made a small sound in her throat. \'93No Gospel Music?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s hand went to her belly. \'93The original Grayling warranty said never play gospel music to an Elvis, but they\'92re emancipated. They\'92re no one\'92s property. Why would the High Elvis care about the  warranty now? \'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Have you ever heard of an Elvis listening to gospel music?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah thought of all the karaoke bars she\'92d visited. \'93No. I can\'92t think of a single time.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy almost laughed, then didn\'92t. \'93If the High Elvis is joking, it\'92s the sort of joke that ends with a riot and a press release.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy felt the sharp, chemical urge for another patch and ignored it. Not yet. She wasn\'92t going to be the one in the room who looked like she was melting.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley tapped her comms band once, twice. \'93Escort says ten minutes.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy stood, grabbed her handbag, and did a quick inventory with her fingers like a gambler checking chips. Patch packet. A spare patch packet in case she met someone else\'92s crisis and decided to wear it for them. She hesitated, then added a small bottle of water because she\'92d learned the hard way that righteous meetings dehydrated you.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Calling it an office was generous. It was a suite built to look like someone\'92s idea of authority: a dais, a semicircle of chairs arranged like a tribunal, and a wall display that could probably show the weather in heaven if you paid for the lux subscription..\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis stood at the centre of it, full regalia and full exhaustion. There were dark smudges under his eyes, and his cape hung like it was heavy with bad news. Behind him, a row of cabinet Elvi hovered\'97jumpsuits subdued, rhinestones minimal, faces drawn.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At the far side of the room, three men in robes sat with the patient stillness of people who had invented time as a concept and then gotten bored with it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Sages.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A moment of silence settled\'97heavy, deliberate. Kathy could hear the hum of electronics and became intensely aware of her own breathing, and of how stupid it was that breathing could suddenly feel like a risky behaviour.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis spoke first, his voice quiet but carrying. \'93In the last forty-eight hours,\'94 he said, \'93across multiple venues\'97bars, nightclubs, ice cream parlours, burger joints \'85 jukeboxes have been sabotaged.\'94 He glanced across to an Elvine cabinet minister, who became uncomfortably aware she was expected to finish the thought.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Someone\'92s been replacing a number of records in the jukeboxes with\'85\'94 and here she swallowed hard. \'93Gospel music,\'94 she eventually blurted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A cabinet Elvis cleared his throat and looked down at his hands. His sideburns were conspicuously short.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Across the room, Chilo tilted his head, as if listening to a distant broadcast that no one else could hear. Kleoboulos didn\'92t move at all.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley broke the silence. \'93So \'85 what?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis\'92s shoulders rose and fell. He looked, for a moment, like a man trapped inside a costume he could no longer take off. \'93Every Elvis who hears a gospel song becomes a blubbering mess. They renounce every thing that makes Elvi culture so \'85 Elvis.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They\'92re giving up sex and drugs\'85\'94 the Elvine cabinet minister said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Gratuitous violence towards TV sets and bathroom fixtures,\'94 said another.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Some of them are even becoming \'85 
\f3\i vegetarians
\f1\i0 ,\'94 The High Elvis added.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And teetotallers,\'94 another cabinet member almost spat.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Some say it\'92s sin to shake your hips, for tax sake.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis chimed back in. \'93The worst part is, they\'92re trimming their sideburns.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Even Kathy was appalled at that. \'93Even the Elvines?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes!\'94 another of the cabinet Elvines blurted, her own feminine facial hair perfectly sculpted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They are joining a sect calling themselves the Holy Rollers,\'94 The High Elvis said. \'93And gathering in places that used to be ours.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A murmur rippled through the cabinet\'97fear dressed up as decorum.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We have always fought,\'94 the High Elvis said. \'93We fight about lyrics. We fight about hair styles. We fight about rhinestones versus sequins.\'94 His voice sharpened. \'93But this is different. This is\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Memetic,\'94 Kathy said, before she could stop herself.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Every head turned.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy held her smile and wished she hadn\'92t opened her mouth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo\'92s eyes gleamed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis closed his eyes and nodded. \'93Yes,\'94 he said. \'93Memetic. Their message spreads by song.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis pointed at the Sages, expecting.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleoboulos inclined his head. \'93We have methods.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Methods that do not harm my people?\'94 the High Elvis said, and the room went colder. \'93They are not to be harmed.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy watched the Sages\'92 faces. She didn\'92t trust them. She didn\'92t trust anyone who sat that still.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo smiled mildly, like a man being asked to do something inconvenient and possibly humiliating with a combine harvester.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Holy Momma,\'94 The High Elvis added, quieter, \'93What I have to say, almost breaks my heart. It hurts me so bad, I almost don\'92t care about coiffing my own hair.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s breath caught. Her belly tightened\'97sharp, unmistakable. She didn\'92t make a sound, just rode it out with her jaw set, as if giving pain any attention would encourage it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy watched Sarah suffering abnormally intense Braxton Hicks\'97more evidence this pregnancy was anything but normal. She feared the foetal implant would complicate the birth, and blinked away visions of Little Clinton busting out of Sarah\'92s belly rather than simply tearing her perineum.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis\'92s hands clenched on the arms of his chair. \'93The Holy Rollers say you are temptation,\'94 he said. \'93They say your child is a test.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy felt heat rise behind her eyes. \'93A test?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They know about the Dawkins, the advocate for evolution over religion. They know your child is a clone for science.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He\'92s just\'85 he\'92s not even born yet!\'94 Sarah protested.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A silence.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley spoke for the first time, voice level and sharp. \'93Who swapped the records?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis\'92s cabinet looked away. In a room full of leaders, no one wanted to be the first to say, \'93we don\'92t know.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo\'92s smile widened by a millimetre.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy watched him and felt very clearly that the Sages were enjoying this.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis\'92s voice went very quiet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I do not care who did it,\'94 he said. \'93Not yet. I care what we do now.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He looked at Kathy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You are a healer,\'94 he said, as if it were an accusation. \'93Can you cure it?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy\'92s mouth went dry.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She thought of Sarah\'92s dreams. Of the patterns she saw like language.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I doubt it,\'94 Kathy said honestly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis exhaled, long and steady, as if he\'92d been holding his breath for two days.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Then,\'94 he said, \'93we will find out.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He straightened in his chair, cape settling behind him like a verdict.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Wise ones,\'94 he said to the Sages. \'93Prepare your ancient methods.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Then, to Kathy and Sarah and Hayley: \'93You help me protect my people, help us reverse the scourge of gospel music.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy stared at him. She didn\'92t know whether to laugh or vomit. She reached into her bag, felt the reassuring edge of a patch packet, and said the only thing she could think of that wasn\'92t a hymn.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tax it,\'94 she murmured. \'93All right.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And somewhere in the building, faint as a rumour, someone began to hum.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A gospel tune.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Every Elvis present covered their ears, shut their eyes, and clamped their jaws shut. Kathy could only hope, like most songs in the cracked universe, that they could never agree on the correct words. She wondered what Chanta would write.\
